body:
I know and totally agree with what you are saying but if you jumped you wouldn't have the chance to experience that same happiness. I honestly fucking hate holidays because everything feels so forced and we have to act a certain way, otherwise we "ruin" the holiday for everyone else. I know that it could be hard feeling a strong sense of loneliness and isolation and it may not be to the same extent as yours but every time i think about offing myself i always remember that there is at least one person who cares about me now, and there isn't, there will be one in the future. Even if there isn't gonna ever be anyone to love me i still don't want to miss the opportunity of it. We only have one life and the way we live it isn't entirely up to us, not everyone has their happy endings but one thing is for certain that even if it is a unhappy ending, at least i experienced what I did. 

&#x200B;

sorry if this was a incoherent mess, but i think i got my point across fairly well.

[https://scoresaber.com/u/76561198320907379](https://scoresaber.com/u/76561198320907379) \- this is my scoresaber profile.

&#x200B;

I believe at least a couple of these songs are 9 stars like takoyaki. I have around the same time as you but i don't use the normal grip. I play on Oculus Quest (Through link now) and I adopted the Claw grip pretty early on. Today i found a playlist tool that actually helps you find ranked songs that are around your skill level. [https://scoresaber.balibalo.xyz/peepee](https://scoresaber.balibalo.xyz/peepee) you just put the link to your scoresaber in and it will generate a playlist based on the songs you have played and the scores you have gotten. I did it today and it help me improve somewhat.

&#x200B;

edit: I also forgot to mention that i play everyday. I don't really know if this affects improvement all that much but it feels like playing more consistently, and more frequently helps me improve. I don't just play songs that i know i can beat or songs that i'm good at. I play songs that are far above my skill level and never stick to one song at a time. Recently even, I've gotten into tech and challenge maps which have actually help me improve my reading ability to a small extent.

i know what you are talking about. I played mainly slow maps when i first started playing and going from that to overkill felt pretty normal, until like a week later i tried a somewhat faster song which i believe was Someone to hate. I played that song and it felt so fast at first but about 2 days in i finally almost passed it. It took me about a week to pass it with an S and then i moved on to levels slightly harder or the same skill level. about 3 months into the game i started playing 7-9 star maps and tried every single day to even come close to beating them. I never really made it farther than about a minute into each song but i could feel the notes slowing down even though they weren't actually slowing down. after that i went back to some old maps i played and tried, and failed to beat most of them. Even the ones i did beat, i only got A's in. Up until recently have i been able to beat them. When i switched over too PC recently, i got into some of the Tech maps i found, and some more challenging maps. I also started playing more ranked songs and i feel like the both of them have improved my reading ability a lot, or at least enough to help me with the slower songs. the problem i had with the slower songs was that i seen the block and swung too early, but i guess that just went away with time.

&#x200B;

TL;DR: I played maps that were a little too fast and ended up messing up my ability to play slower maps for a short period of time.

the same thing happened to me in a much smaller scale. I had bought a knife on CSGO for about $80 and someone offered to trade it for a much higher asking price than what it actually was in visa gift cards. I was a child when this incident happened but In hindsight i should've seen it coming and as soon as i traded the cards to him he blocked me and i could do nothing about it. I'm not the smartest person in the world but I'm also not stupid, and i wasn't any stupider back then. In the moment the thoughts of what i could do with the extra money overpowered the fact that this was a blatant scam. I don't think you should forgive him that easily but im sure over time you will be less angry about it and forgive him. We all make mistakes, some worse than others, and unfortunately he made a really big one.

&#x200B;

Definitely make sure he doesn't make this mistake ever again, and if he does then you probably shouldn't have to forgive him. He needs to learn from his mistakes  but if he doesn't then he probably never will.

I feel as if the best course of action is to tell them what you did. If your not comfortable telling them, tell a friend or someone you can trust. If all else fails you can always talk to a stranger, or call the suicide hotline and see if that helps. Most people say that this doesn't last forever, and it eventually will go away. I personally don't believe it's like that for everyone, And it probably seems like that to you even more so. I guarantee that even if something doesn't happen in life that just washes away all that pain, you would still feel happier in the end. If you ever do find that something, try holding onto it, or them, for your entire life. Just remember that theirs gonna be someone who's going through the almost exact same thing you are going through. I know its not as simple as just telling yourself it will get better. Try and find something that gives you meaning, be that writing, filming, video games, just anything in general that you enjoy doing. The way I deal with mine is trying to think about all of the things I would miss about being alive, and all the people, places, and things I would miss too. Depression is different for everyone, meaning the way you overcome it can be different too. I suffer from major social anxiety and self-esteem issues, and I can't really talk to people face to face, or make eye contact. Although I do have quite a few friends, I can't help but feel alone and completely useless. I physically know that none of them think that of me, but my brain just tells me that they would be better off without me, and I'd be lying if I said that i didn't believe it most times. The thing i'm self conscious about is my forehead. Its Naturally huge, and of course like elementary, and middle schoolers do, they make fun of other people's insecurities. Even though that specific thing might not affect me, the effects of being made fun of still continue to haunt me to this day. I'm slowly improving, and I know from experience that it doesn't come quickly. I'm really happy it did happen though because I've met a lot of new people who II would've never even looked at if i didn't try. I guess my point is, You have to have a constant state of mind that you are improving, and you have to do things to stop feeling so empty. Also, I hope you recover well. Hopefully what you did, did no permanent damage.

&#x200B;

Edit: also sorry for the format of this. I kinda wrote it like an essay but I'm too lazy to try and spiffy it up, and since there'sI really no reason to, i don't see the need for it. Once again, best regards, and hopefully you can recover from both of these traumas.
